00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:31.030
and optimize our
architecture in order

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:32.238
to meet those usage patterns.

00:00:32.238 --> 00:00:33.988
So what we're going
to talk to you today--

00:00:33.988 --> 00:00:36.220
is how we were able to learn
through experimentation,

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.950
how we were able to
identify opportunities

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:42.190
to either optimize for cost
or optimize for customer

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:44.950
performance, and how
those lessons learned

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:47.740
have influenced how we're
transforming our business

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:51.700
and future-proofing in future
data-driven developments.

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.115
Go on to the next.

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:59.360
Dow Jones had an over
30-year of premium news data

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.230
that existed only in
on-premise infrastructure.

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:05.272
What we discovered
is that it met

00:01:05.272 --> 00:01:07.730
the need of our existing use
cases, which are predominantly

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:09.920
around advanced
research and advanced

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.840
analytics, predominately
focused at research librarians.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.130
But we were getting
increasingly frequent requests

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:18.110
for extractions of data.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:20.720
And that was actually
really challenging in

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:22.620
our on-premise infrastructure.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:25.640
So what the data engineering
team was charged with doing

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:28.340
was actually doing the
migration to the cloud

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:31.850
to support existing use cases
for the advanced research,

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:35.090
new use cases for machine
learning and big data

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:37.940
workflows, as well as
being innovative enough

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:40.465
to be future-proofing
for whatever is next.

00:01:40.465 --> 00:01:42.590
So Dylan, what were some
of the original challenges

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:45.450
in making this data
available to customers?

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.450
DYLAN ROY: So for this
new class of customers,

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:49.770
these data scientists,
they required more data.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:55.040
So we were trying to
fulfill their requests,

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.830
but they needed more data than
we needed for the researchers.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:00.590
So with this on-prem
database, we'd

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:03.290
have to give them
the data on weekends

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:06.350
or over the low-traffic
times that we

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:09.252
had for our research product.

00:02:09.252 --> 00:02:10.460
But that just wasn't working.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:12.793
We weren't able to fulfill
the requests of our customers

00:02:12.793 --> 00:02:14.660
that were needing this data.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:18.370
As a result, we were
actually posed a question.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:21.060
Are we going to stay
on-prem and try to scale up?

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.580
Or are we going to
move to the cloud

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:26.370
where we can massively
scale up ephemerally

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:29.460
like with ephemeral clouds?

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:33.980
So due to this lack of
bandwidth and this lack of scale

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:38.150
and the size of our team, we
decided to move to the cloud,

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:41.990
specifically, the Google Cloud
because they gave us zero-ops

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:42.850
options--

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:45.140
I have some listed here--

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:49.070
like Dataflow,
Dataproc, and also GCS.

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:53.640
I'll go over those when we go
over the architecture later.

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:54.580
So yeah.

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:57.050
I'm just going to hit home
again what the pros and cons are

00:02:57.050 --> 00:02:58.700
for moving to the cloud.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:00.800
You can scale massively,
especially for these data

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.300
science workloads
where you're going

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:05.460
to need large clusters
to process all this data.

00:03:05.460 --> 00:03:07.430
For us, it's over
30 years of news

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:11.807
content from over
33,000 news sources.

00:03:11.807 --> 00:03:13.640
It also allows for
smaller teams because you

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:17.270
have these managed services
that you can take advantage of.

00:03:17.270 --> 00:03:20.510
It also allows to give you
disaster recovery capabilities

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:22.520
that previously
were not available.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:26.150
Because, on-prem, we only had
a limited number of locations.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:29.860
With GCP or with cloud offerings
that we have available to us

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:32.150
now, we can be located
in different countries

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:34.690
throughout the world.

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:37.610
And given this
upfront cost, we still

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:40.180
wanted to move
forward to the cloud.

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:41.750
So I'll bring it
over to Patricia

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:45.910
to continue us on our
journey after I step us

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:48.150
through the architecture here.

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:51.590
So here is our
simplified architecture.

00:03:51.590 --> 00:03:56.750
What we had was a news content
feed on the right side there.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:59.810
It's kind of shown
as a Mario work pipe.

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:05.028
And that fed into
GCS for storage.

00:04:05.028 --> 00:04:07.070
And everyday, we'd have
a Dataflow job that would

00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:09.400
process all those news events.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:12.770
They were updates, deletes,
or additions of articles.

00:04:12.770 --> 00:04:16.670
And that would be pushed
into to Datastore.

00:04:16.670 --> 00:04:20.089
From there, our customers
interact via an API.

00:04:20.089 --> 00:04:24.470
But this API, we had
to apply custom DSLs.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:26.390
So that was problematic
along with the fact

00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:30.380
that it was pretty
expensive off the cuff here.

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:33.718
And I'll let Patricia
let us continue on.

00:04:33.718 --> 00:04:35.510
PATRICIA WALSH: So once
the 30-year archive

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.990
was moved to the cloud,
what we wanted to enable

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:42.560
was really the opportunity to
integrate our data with machine

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:44.790
learning workflows as
well as big data workflow.

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:48.620
So we identified two
delivery mechanisms

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:51.110
that we wanted to enable
via cloud services.

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:53.540
First delivery mechanism was
what's called a snapshot.

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:56.270
And that's a downloadable
extraction of data,

00:04:56.270 --> 00:04:59.720
a custom data set, for whatever
is the specific use case that

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.750
can be either transferred
to a cloud provider

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:04.610
to work an existing workflows.

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:07.910
Or it can be downloaded to
any enterprise customers

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:09.890
on-premise behind a firewall.

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:13.010
So if privacy is an issue,
we're able to meet that need.

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:15.500
The second delivery
mechanism we enabled

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:16.790
was what's called a stream.

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:20.750
And that's a custom
filter of incoming news,

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:23.510
near real-time ingestion,
that can either be used

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:25.340
to keep a Datastore up to date.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.980
Or it can be used for
things like risk target

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:32.000
identification, brand
management at scale,

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.380
due diligence at scale.

00:05:33.380 --> 00:05:34.910
So these are the
delivery mechanisms

00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:38.210
we wanted to investigate and
make available via the DNA

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:39.200
platform.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.890
What we discovered early on
in our initial experimentation

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:46.190
with cloud architecture is
that the usage pattern--

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:48.170
moving to the cloud gives
us better opportunity

00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:51.200
to track and identify and
understand our usage patterns.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:53.030
And our architecture
needs to be adjusted

00:05:53.030 --> 00:05:55.260
to meet the needs of
our usage patterns.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:57.990
And we identified
trade-off decisions

00:05:57.990 --> 00:06:00.180
that were either
to optimize cost

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:03.598
or to optimize the
performance for our customer.

00:06:03.598 --> 00:06:05.640
One opportunity-- so what
we're going to do today

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:08.700
is we're going to use this
balance as an analogy for what

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:13.200
is either the optimization of
cost that's enabled via cloud

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.350
services or the
opportunity to improve

00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:19.740
your performance
to your customers

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:21.900
by leveraging cloud services.

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:26.130
Cost optimizations-- if we were
going to improve our cost--

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:28.480
for example, in the
use case of a snapshot,

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:30.720
which is a downloadable
extraction,

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:33.760
what we identified as the usage
pattern is very consistent.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:36.900
So these are workloads that can
be done in batch processing.

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:39.390
These are workloads that
can withstand interruption.

00:06:39.390 --> 00:06:40.950
So what we have
is, we could make

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:43.950
an investment in
cost optimization

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:46.500
by using preemptable instances.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:49.380
We could invest in
cost optimization

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:53.010
by investing in tiered
storage, by leveraging

00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:54.990
the advantages of tier storage.

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:58.980
You could do this the same
with sustained use discounts.

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:01.650
And you'll notice, as we
leverage the cost optimization,

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:06.540
the performance may degradate.

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:11.880
So as you're optimizing,
either for cost or performance,

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:14.340
the key is not necessarily
finding equilibrium.

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:16.740
It's striking the
right balance of cost

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:19.297
to performance for
that specific use case.

00:07:19.297 --> 00:07:20.880
Now, streams, on the
other hand, which

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:24.150
is a near real-time
filter of data, well,

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:28.080
that we would really
heavily weight being highly

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:29.730
available on being reliable.

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:34.230
In that event, we'd really want
to invest in managed services.

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:38.700
And we'd want to invest
in read/write access.

00:07:38.700 --> 00:07:41.650
And we'd also want to invest
in analytics capabilities.

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:43.390
So in investing in
our performance,

00:07:43.390 --> 00:07:46.090
the performance time goes
down, but the costs may go up.

00:07:46.090 --> 00:07:47.730
So it's really, as
I mentioned, never

00:07:47.730 --> 00:07:51.060
a matter of trying to
strike equilibrium.

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:53.250
It's a matter of trying
to find the balance that

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:55.750
meets your specific use case.

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:58.290
So Dylan, once the data was
made available on the cloud,

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:00.582
can you walk us through some
of the cost to performance

00:08:00.582 --> 00:08:03.863
trade-offs that you
discovered with Datastore?

00:08:03.863 --> 00:08:04.530
DYLAN ROY: Yeah.

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:07.455
So with Datastore, we were
giving high availability

00:08:07.455 --> 00:08:08.738
of reads and writes.

00:08:08.738 --> 00:08:11.280
And also, it integrates really
well with our pipelining tool,

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:13.650
Dataflow.

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:16.993
It also allows us to scale a
lot better than we did on-prem.

00:08:16.993 --> 00:08:19.285
Some of the cons were, it's
very expensive for the read

00:08:19.285 --> 00:08:23.040
and write operations,
especially for the usage

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.017
patterns we were seeing.

00:08:25.017 --> 00:08:26.850
On top of that, we had
to create our own DSL

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:29.370
and educate our
customers on that DSL.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:33.669
So there was a better way
for us to move forward.

00:08:33.669 --> 00:08:34.440
And Patricia?

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:35.520
PATRICIA WALSH: Yeah.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:37.440
So for the product
of snapshots, we

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:39.120
do require analytics
capabilities,

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.880
but we also require the
read/write capabilities.

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:45.660
Datastore proved to really
meet the need for analytics

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:49.080
for the performance
expectation of our customers,

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:53.510
but did not meet the
cost-performant needs.

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:55.260
So Dylan, what were
some of the advantages

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:57.890
of moving towards BigQuery
as our search index?

00:08:57.890 --> 00:08:58.770
DYLAN ROY: Yeah.

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:01.800
So I'll explain the
architecture and then

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:03.600
bring to light the
optimizations we

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:06.870
made that made this a better
architecture than the one

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:09.280
that we had for Datastore.

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:13.440
And I'm going to explain it
from right to left starting

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:15.720
with the Mario
pipeline of content.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:19.260
And so, yeah, that's
still feeding in into GCS

00:09:19.260 --> 00:09:22.965
with those events that we're
using to update our archive.

00:09:22.965 --> 00:09:24.840
And then we still have
that Dataflow pipeline

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:27.780
that updates daily,
except the difference here

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:31.480
is we separated our query
layer from our storage layer.

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:33.510
And for our query
layer, we use BigQuery.

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:35.550
And BigQuery allows
us to have that SQL--

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:39.090
the standard SQL syntax that we
can use and allow our customers

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:40.990
to leverage as well.

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:45.250
And also, we have GCS to
store our full set of content.

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:47.185
Another reason we had
to split our query layer

00:09:47.185 --> 00:09:48.810
and our storage layer
was because there

00:09:48.810 --> 00:09:51.090
were some limitations
with BigQuery,

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:54.240
specifically, the extraction
limits that we ran into.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:57.620
We do not run into this with
the current architecture.

00:09:57.620 --> 00:10:00.240
And I'll kind of bring that to
light through the customer's

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:03.690
journey when they submit
a request for a snapshot.

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:06.080
And that can be seen
here on the right side.

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:09.210
Our customers interact
with the APIs.

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:11.650
And what they're requesting
is a snapshot of data.

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:13.740
And once they submit
that request--

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:17.040
an example request would
be for all English content

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:18.750
that we have in our archive.

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:24.143
And that will kick
off a Dataflow job.

00:10:24.143 --> 00:10:25.560
And what that
Dataflow job will do

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:28.290
is, it'll go to BigQuery
with that WHERE clause

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:30.770
and grab all the
IDs of the articles

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:33.420
and the identifiers for
the most current version

00:10:33.420 --> 00:10:35.470
of each article.

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:38.520
And from that point,
it'll do a co-group key

00:10:38.520 --> 00:10:43.080
or just sort of join with the
data we have stored on GCS.

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:47.595
From that point, once it has
all the content identified,

00:10:47.595 --> 00:10:49.920
it will then output the
snapshot to a location

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:52.740
that the customer can
use to download it.

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:54.530
But that's still
done via our APIs.

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:56.300
And our customer
still has no idea

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:59.630
that we're no longer
using Datastore.

00:10:59.630 --> 00:11:01.280
So that's the architecture.

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:04.903
And I'll kind of reiterate
the pros and cons of this.

00:11:04.903 --> 00:11:06.320
So with these
resources that we're

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:08.740
spinning up with Dataflow,
they're ephemeral.

00:11:08.740 --> 00:11:12.030
So we're not incurring
that cost as time goes on.

00:11:12.030 --> 00:11:14.270
We're only incurring
the cost while we're

00:11:14.270 --> 00:11:17.697
doing the operation, we're
doing the snapshot, for example.

00:11:17.697 --> 00:11:20.030
We're not paying a high price
for updates to the archive

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:24.470
as we were before
with Datastore.

00:11:24.470 --> 00:11:26.080
Also, we get that
free query syntax

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:29.660
that BigQuery gives us with SQL.

00:11:29.660 --> 00:11:31.648
And GCS is very cheap.

00:11:31.648 --> 00:11:33.440
So it's the perfect
place to store our data

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:35.910
when we're not using it.

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:37.520
So with this
architecture, we still

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:39.530
have that very expensive
joining process

00:11:39.530 --> 00:11:43.460
with the query layer
and the storage layer.

00:11:43.460 --> 00:11:45.530
And we also need
a separate cluster

00:11:45.530 --> 00:11:48.360
for each and every operation.

00:11:48.360 --> 00:11:49.740
So there may be a better way.

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:53.360
And you may see that
further on in our talk here.

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:55.790
PATRICIA WALSH: So in
moving to using BigQuery

00:11:55.790 --> 00:12:00.680
for our analytics, we were
able to do big workflows

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:03.265
at a relatively inexpensive--

00:12:03.265 --> 00:12:04.640
BigQuery's relatively
inexpensive

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:05.935
for analytics capabilities.

00:12:05.935 --> 00:12:07.310
But we hit some
quotas and we hit

00:12:07.310 --> 00:12:08.910
some limits when it
came to extraction,

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:11.160
which is why we then paired
with Google Cloud Storage.

00:12:11.160 --> 00:12:13.112
We really used the
cost optimizations

00:12:13.112 --> 00:12:15.320
of the tiered storage with
the analytics capabilities

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:16.620
of BigQuery.

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:18.650
So Dylan, in our
snapshots architecture,

00:12:18.650 --> 00:12:21.640
why was the move made
from Dataflow to Dataproc?

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:22.970
DYLAN ROY: OK, yeah.

00:12:22.970 --> 00:12:25.160
I'll explain this
architecture, and you

00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:27.170
may see the cons
kind of bubble up,

00:12:27.170 --> 00:12:31.620
or what no longer are cons
with this architecture.

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:35.990
So here, we still have that
Mario pipeline of content

00:12:35.990 --> 00:12:41.355
going into GCS, updating daily,
and going into the content

00:12:41.355 --> 00:12:42.230
archive we have here.

00:12:42.230 --> 00:12:46.110
That's a little
different, as you may see.

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:49.250
The customer is still
submitting API requests.

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:51.710
But when the customer
submits an API request

00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:54.230
for all English
content this time,

00:12:54.230 --> 00:12:57.230
they're just going to
be having a job spin up.

00:12:57.230 --> 00:13:00.650
And we can use Dataproc
and their SQL syntax

00:13:00.650 --> 00:13:03.650
to create this snapshot
of content that'll

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:05.930
be delivered to our customer.

00:13:05.930 --> 00:13:08.270
There were some cons
with this method.

00:13:08.270 --> 00:13:11.480
But I'll go over those
after I go over the pros,

00:13:11.480 --> 00:13:13.620
because this is a good thing.

00:13:13.620 --> 00:13:17.220
So with this architecture, we
were able to share resources.

00:13:17.220 --> 00:13:20.660
So when we had two customers
make a snapshot request,

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:23.990
we can still use that
same archive of content.

00:13:23.990 --> 00:13:28.100
With Dataflow, we'd have to
load into our cluster each

00:13:28.100 --> 00:13:29.630
and every time.

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:34.830
Here, we can mount the same
drive and the same content.

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:38.360
So this also more closely
aligns with our usage patterns.

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:41.780
We have more and more customers
join and use our platform.

00:13:41.780 --> 00:13:45.110
And because of that, we can
use these higher-cost resources

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:48.180
when it comes to
doing the operations.

00:13:48.180 --> 00:13:49.852
There's also no need
for that joining

00:13:49.852 --> 00:13:51.560
of the query layer
and the storage layer,

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:55.700
because we're using Spark
on Dataproc which has a SQL

00:13:55.700 --> 00:13:58.370
syntax that we can leverage.

00:13:58.370 --> 00:14:02.120
But with this, there is still
more operational overhead.

00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:05.600
With Dataflow, it can recover
from errors or any issue

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:07.220
with the cluster.

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:09.530
With Dataproc, we still
have to build in some logic

00:14:09.530 --> 00:14:14.090
to kind of restart the cluster
and recover from errors.

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:16.430
On top of that, when
we moved from BigQuery

00:14:16.430 --> 00:14:19.520
and went to the
Spark syntax, we had

00:14:19.520 --> 00:14:24.640
to create some UDFs to bridge
that gap between functionality.

00:14:24.640 --> 00:14:27.290
And for those of
you interested, it

00:14:27.290 --> 00:14:31.933
was related to
regular expressions.

00:14:31.933 --> 00:14:33.350
And I'll throw it
over to Patricia

00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:35.340
to continue us on this journey.

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:37.500
PATRICIA WALSH: So we have
a relatively small team.

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:40.970
We started with two engineers,
and we now have five engineers.

00:14:40.970 --> 00:14:43.490
Through the experimentation,
in moving to the cloud,

00:14:43.490 --> 00:14:46.100
we were able to identify that
the usage pattern for snapshots

00:14:46.100 --> 00:14:48.570
is very consistent and
it's very predictable.

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:51.710
So it was a worthwhile
trade-off to go to the increased

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:55.610
overhead of an unmanaged
service as it gave us

00:14:55.610 --> 00:14:58.310
increasing control over
the expensive operations

00:14:58.310 --> 00:15:00.350
like joining and windowing.

00:15:00.350 --> 00:15:02.840
So there was a cost benefit
to us that was worth it

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:05.580
and worth taking
on the overhead.

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:10.010
So Dylan, for streams, which
is a near real-time filtered

00:15:10.010 --> 00:15:13.610
stream of data where highly
available services is weighted

00:15:13.610 --> 00:15:16.160
heavier, why was the
choice made to move

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:19.963
from Dataflow to Dataproc,
or Dataproc to Dataflow?

00:15:19.963 --> 00:15:20.630
DYLAN ROY: Yeah.

00:15:20.630 --> 00:15:21.080
We're going backwards
[INAUDIBLE]..

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:24.017
PATRICIA WALSH: We've had that
same conversation 1,000 times.

00:15:24.017 --> 00:15:24.850
DYLAN ROY: OK, yeah.

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:27.032
With the streams
architecture, our customers

00:15:27.032 --> 00:15:28.490
are going to notice
if it goes down

00:15:28.490 --> 00:15:31.460
for a few minutes or seconds,
unlike with snapshots.

00:15:31.460 --> 00:15:34.340
They're not going to notice
because those operations are

00:15:34.340 --> 00:15:37.193
going to take between
an hour or two hours.

00:15:37.193 --> 00:15:39.800
This, it's low
latency, so they're

00:15:39.800 --> 00:15:43.640
going to notice when messages
are not streaming to them.

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:46.160
With this, we're still hooking
into that stream of content

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:48.410
that's coming from
the Mario tube.

00:15:48.410 --> 00:15:52.310
But it's streaming on
to pub/sub right now.

00:15:52.310 --> 00:15:55.790
And we're still leveraging
the Dataflow services

00:15:55.790 --> 00:15:57.980
to do our ETL processes.

00:15:57.980 --> 00:15:59.830
And the great thing
about Dataflow

00:15:59.830 --> 00:16:01.920
is you can write job for
a batch or streaming job,

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:04.715
and it's going to be
either the same or similar.

00:16:04.715 --> 00:16:06.090
So we're taking
advantage of some

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:07.950
of the code we
wrote for snapshots

00:16:07.950 --> 00:16:10.680
and using it in streams
to filter in and update

00:16:10.680 --> 00:16:12.600
our content.

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:16.890
From that point, we filter it
into different product types

00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:20.790
because we have
content that's licensed

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:22.510
to only certain products.

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:26.370
And we use Dataflow to
filter that content.

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:29.370
From there, this is
where our customers

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:30.990
interact with our system.

00:16:30.990 --> 00:16:33.900
They use our user APIs as if
they would be using a snapshot.

00:16:33.900 --> 00:16:35.610
They submit the same query.

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:37.440
Like, if they want
all English content,

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:39.690
they'll submit that
little WHERE clause.

00:16:39.690 --> 00:16:45.480
And from that point, we'll
spin up a Dataflow filter.

00:16:45.480 --> 00:16:48.120
Before, though, that
was a Dataproc filter.

00:16:48.120 --> 00:16:50.550
And I'll go into the pros
and cons as to why we changed

00:16:50.550 --> 00:16:54.000
that part of the architecture.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:56.010
So once the customer
has created the stream

00:16:56.010 --> 00:16:58.560
and they have their custom
set of content streaming

00:16:58.560 --> 00:17:02.670
to them on this
pub/sub topic here,

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:04.728
they can then use
our client code

00:17:04.728 --> 00:17:06.270
and get streamed
all the content that

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:08.730
gets filtered down to them.

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:12.750
But we had to make that move
that I was talking earlier,

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:14.970
from Dataproc to Dataflow.

00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:18.119
And that was because, every
time we would use preemptables

00:17:18.119 --> 00:17:22.260
in our cluster, it would
take down our stream,

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:24.869
and our customers would notice.

00:17:24.869 --> 00:17:27.660
With Dataflow, we
actually have had

00:17:27.660 --> 00:17:31.020
streams that have been
running for multiple years.

00:17:31.020 --> 00:17:34.380
Along with that, it allows for
this fully-managed zero-ops

00:17:34.380 --> 00:17:35.160
data processing.

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:37.140
And for our small
team, this is great

00:17:37.140 --> 00:17:39.598
because we don't have to wake
up in the middle of the night

00:17:39.598 --> 00:17:40.500
and handle an issue.

00:17:40.500 --> 00:17:44.038
It also allows for more
seamless auto-scaling,

00:17:44.038 --> 00:17:45.330
which helps with smaller teams.

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:49.110
And with streams, there was
not heavy load, as we only

00:17:49.110 --> 00:17:54.290
receive 800,000 messages a day.

00:17:54.290 --> 00:17:57.300
In big data land,
that's not a lot.

00:17:57.300 --> 00:17:57.810
So yeah.

00:17:57.810 --> 00:18:00.227
There were the cons of not
being able to use preemptables,

00:18:00.227 --> 00:18:01.270
which were very cheap.

00:18:01.270 --> 00:18:05.380
If you do use Dataproc, I
suggest looking into it.

00:18:05.380 --> 00:18:08.490
And also, we had to leverage
an in-memory database

00:18:08.490 --> 00:18:10.260
for the queries.

00:18:10.260 --> 00:18:13.120
We didn't have the BigQuery
syntax or the Spark syntax

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:16.770
that we could plug
into and use SQL.

00:18:16.770 --> 00:18:19.260
But if I were to do
this again, Dataflow now

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:24.020
has a SQL DSL, which
we have not used.

00:18:24.020 --> 00:18:25.520
I'll throw it over
to you, Patricia.

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:26.760
PATRICIA WALSH: Thanks.

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:32.440
So sort of to summarize some
of the lessons learned--

00:18:32.440 --> 00:18:34.202
the usage patterns seem obvious.

00:18:34.202 --> 00:18:36.160
But actually, once we
released it to the world,

00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:37.700
we did have some
APIs that were used

00:18:37.700 --> 00:18:39.340
in ways we hadn't intended.

00:18:39.340 --> 00:18:42.310
And all of a sudden,
your costs skyrocket,

00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:44.380
and you start becoming
very painfully

00:18:44.380 --> 00:18:46.460
aware of where you have
opportunity to optimize

00:18:46.460 --> 00:18:48.660
and where you have
opportunity to change.

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:51.970
And so what I would
recommend and advise is,

00:18:51.970 --> 00:18:54.550
initially, we were
always optimizing

00:18:54.550 --> 00:18:56.660
for performant responses.

00:18:56.660 --> 00:19:00.880
And if you invest too heavily
in one side of the trade-offs,

00:19:00.880 --> 00:19:02.650
you fall over on
top of yourself.

00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:04.420
If you're only
investing in one side,

00:19:04.420 --> 00:19:06.610
you get skewed off
balance really quickly.

00:19:06.610 --> 00:19:08.290
And that becomes very
apparent when you

00:19:08.290 --> 00:19:11.080
get the bill for the service.

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:14.830
We had a use case that ended up
being, like, a $70,000 use case

00:19:14.830 --> 00:19:17.150
that we were not expecting.

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:19.120
So what we really
learned is-- and anytime

00:19:19.120 --> 00:19:21.640
we do a feature development--
to first identify the usage

00:19:21.640 --> 00:19:24.190
pattern, identify the
behavior, really understand,

00:19:24.190 --> 00:19:26.810
from your users, how
they're intending to use it,

00:19:26.810 --> 00:19:29.727
which might be different than
your original assumption.

00:19:29.727 --> 00:19:31.810
And then once you do
understand the usage pattern,

00:19:31.810 --> 00:19:35.062
to build in such a flexible
way that you can understand

00:19:35.062 --> 00:19:36.520
the trade-off
decisions you've made

00:19:36.520 --> 00:19:38.740
versus the performant
experience for your customer

00:19:38.740 --> 00:19:41.620
and your opportunities
that the cloud enables you

00:19:41.620 --> 00:19:42.965
for costs optimizations.

00:19:42.965 --> 00:19:44.590
So Dylan, what were
some of the lessons

00:19:44.590 --> 00:19:48.160
you learned in making this new
product available on Google

00:19:48.160 --> 00:19:50.050
Cloud?

00:19:50.050 --> 00:19:50.740
DYLAN ROY: Yeah.

00:19:50.740 --> 00:19:52.907
One thing that we found
useful as we were developing

00:19:52.907 --> 00:19:55.630
this new product that we've
not built before was we

00:19:55.630 --> 00:19:58.210
built time in our roadmap
to iterate and spike

00:19:58.210 --> 00:20:01.220
our new infrastructure
when we could.

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:05.710
Google's always developing new
zero-ops tools that you can use

00:20:05.710 --> 00:20:07.870
and other cloud
providers are as well.

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:10.780
We actually started on a
different cloud provider

00:20:10.780 --> 00:20:13.000
before we moved to Google.

00:20:13.000 --> 00:20:15.430
Also, we learned that
being an early adopter

00:20:15.430 --> 00:20:16.640
is a double-edged sword.

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:18.575
You can guide the roadmap
for their products.

00:20:18.575 --> 00:20:20.950
But, at the same time, you
can use one of their products,

00:20:20.950 --> 00:20:24.190
and it can tip over in a
way you would not previously

00:20:24.190 --> 00:20:25.720
have expected.

00:20:25.720 --> 00:20:29.055
Also, Google very tightly--

00:20:29.055 --> 00:20:32.650
they keep their quotas down
to a limit that prevent you

00:20:32.650 --> 00:20:35.020
from incurring a lot of
costs, but at the same time,

00:20:35.020 --> 00:20:36.603
it could cause an
issue in the future.

00:20:36.603 --> 00:20:38.500
So it's good to know
your quotas especially

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:41.350
when you're an early adopter.

00:20:41.350 --> 00:20:43.090
So yeah.

00:20:43.090 --> 00:20:45.730
This can actually be boiled
down to three things when you're

00:20:45.730 --> 00:20:48.110
deciding on your architecture.

00:20:48.110 --> 00:20:49.450
The first one is cost.

00:20:49.450 --> 00:20:52.690
The larger you can make that
margin for costs of operations,

00:20:52.690 --> 00:20:56.260
the quicker you can grow your
team and iterate on features.

00:20:56.260 --> 00:20:58.455
Another one is team size.

00:20:58.455 --> 00:20:59.830
There are some
architectures that

00:20:59.830 --> 00:21:02.390
may not work for
your small teams,

00:21:02.390 --> 00:21:05.360
especially if you want to have a
team that can develop features.

00:21:05.360 --> 00:21:08.260
And you don't want to be
spending half of that time

00:21:08.260 --> 00:21:09.760
managing infrastructure.

00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:12.670
This is why Google
Dataflow and Dataproc

00:21:12.670 --> 00:21:16.930
have actually come in handy for
us and accelerated our roadmap.

00:21:16.930 --> 00:21:19.450
Another one we kind of
learned trial by fire

00:21:19.450 --> 00:21:21.460
is usage patterns.

00:21:21.460 --> 00:21:23.470
We had a new product
with a new use case.

00:21:23.470 --> 00:21:25.600
And our customers were
using it in a way we did not

00:21:25.600 --> 00:21:26.980
expect them to use it.

00:21:26.980 --> 00:21:29.500
As a result, we took
advantage of the fact

00:21:29.500 --> 00:21:32.830
that we had time in our
roadmap from using these zero

00:21:32.830 --> 00:21:36.010
operational tools
and also the fact

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:39.120
that we built into a roadmap
this time for iterating

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:42.110
on our architecture.

00:21:42.110 --> 00:21:45.510
So luckily, we were able to
adapt and change according

00:21:45.510 --> 00:21:46.510
to these usage patterns.

00:21:46.510 --> 00:21:49.720
But it's very important to
keep a very tight eye on that

00:21:49.720 --> 00:21:52.570
and maybe even log
that data somewhere

00:21:52.570 --> 00:21:55.510
so you can be alerted of when
your costs are kind of spiking,

00:21:55.510 --> 00:21:59.810
and it's time to
address that issue.

00:21:59.810 --> 00:22:01.420
So those are the
lessons I learned.

00:22:01.420 --> 00:22:02.510
PATRICIA WALSH: Yeah.

00:22:02.510 --> 00:22:07.600
So in making this new product
available on the cloud,

00:22:07.600 --> 00:22:09.670
our existing use cases
of advanced research,

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:11.710
we've also now made a
new line of business

00:22:11.710 --> 00:22:14.040
for machine learning
and big data workflows.

00:22:14.040 --> 00:22:16.540
And Dylan's going to walk us
through some of what we've also

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:19.198
enabled in terms of applying
these lessons to even

00:22:19.198 --> 00:22:21.490
future-proofing in some kind
of new and upcoming things

00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:22.920
we've got going on.

00:22:22.920 --> 00:22:23.590
DYLAN ROY: Yeah.

00:22:23.590 --> 00:22:25.365
So this was just
about news content.

00:22:25.365 --> 00:22:27.490
But with news content, you
have different entities.

00:22:27.490 --> 00:22:30.550
You have organizations, you
have people, you have events,

00:22:30.550 --> 00:22:35.930
you have different industries
affecting the news.

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:38.530
So there's a lot of
context that can be gleaned

00:22:38.530 --> 00:22:40.480
from different sets of data.

00:22:40.480 --> 00:22:45.070
And DNA has allowed us to build
on the top of the shoulders

00:22:45.070 --> 00:22:47.230
of this platform
and create products

00:22:47.230 --> 00:22:48.580
that can generate knowledge.

00:22:48.580 --> 00:22:51.310
And actually, you can get a
signal from these news events.

00:22:51.310 --> 00:22:54.040
And we're doing that by
structuring a Knowledge

00:22:54.040 --> 00:22:56.840
Graph, in the way that Google is
doing for their search engine,

00:22:56.840 --> 00:22:59.537
we're going to be doing
for the news and events.

00:22:59.537 --> 00:23:01.870
So we've been able to work
on that, develop new context,

00:23:01.870 --> 00:23:05.830
traverse relations through
our graph architecture.

00:23:05.830 --> 00:23:08.380
And on top of that, if
you're interested in the kind

00:23:08.380 --> 00:23:10.100
of content we've been
dealing with here,

00:23:10.100 --> 00:23:13.300
we have a commercial
data set in BigQuery.

00:23:13.300 --> 00:23:16.400
Just Google BigQuery
commercial data sets DNA,

00:23:16.400 --> 00:23:18.610
and you can take a look
at the news content

00:23:18.610 --> 00:23:20.590
we've been dealing with.

00:23:20.590 --> 00:23:21.190
So yeah.

00:23:21.190 --> 00:23:22.380
[INAUDIBLE]

00:23:22.380 --> 00:23:22.500
PATRICIA WALSH: Yeah.

00:23:22.500 --> 00:23:24.270
So the Knowledge Graph
is the next big thing

00:23:24.270 --> 00:23:25.103
we've got coming up.

00:23:25.103 --> 00:23:27.540
And we're so excited to have
this premium news content,

00:23:27.540 --> 00:23:30.172
but also available with
the entities that exist,

00:23:30.172 --> 00:23:31.880
the relationships
between those entities,

00:23:31.880 --> 00:23:33.812
so you have the
data in the context

00:23:33.812 --> 00:23:36.270
of the data, which is something
we're really excited about.

00:23:36.270 --> 00:23:39.620
[MUSIC PLAYING]

